1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:03,519
Chapter 1

2
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:09,440
Mr. Jones of the Manor Farm had locked the hen houses for the night, but was

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:12,879
too drunk to remember to shut the pop holes.

4
00:00:12,879 --> 00:00:17,679
With the ring of light from his lantern dancing from side to side, he lurched

5
00:00:17,679 --> 00:00:23,119
across the yard, kicked off his boots at the back door, drew himself a last glass

6
00:00:23,119 --> 00:00:28,119
of beer from the barrel in the scullery, and made his way up to bed, where Mrs.

7
00:00:28,119 --> 00:00:31,359
Jones was already snoring.

8
00:00:31,359 --> 00:00:35,240
As soon as the light in the bedroom went out, there was a stirring and a

9
00:00:35,240 --> 00:00:38,240
fluttering all through the farm buildings.

10
00:00:38,240 --> 00:00:43,320
Word had gone round during the day that Old Major, the prize middle white boar,

11
00:00:43,320 --> 00:00:49,799
had had a strange dream on the previous night and wished to communicate it to the other animals.

12
00:00:49,799 --> 00:00:53,799
It had been agreed that they should all meet in the big barn as soon as Mr.

13
00:00:53,799 --> 00:00:56,519
Jones was safely out of the way.

14
00:00:56,519 --> 00:01:01,239
Old Major, so he was always called, though the name under which he had been

15
00:01:01,239 --> 00:01:06,599
exhibited was Willingdon Beauty, was so highly regarded on the farm that

16
00:01:06,599 --> 00:01:11,640
everyone was quite ready to lose an hour's sleep in order to hear what he had to say.

17
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:16,599
At one end of the big barn, on a sort of raised platform, Major was already

18
00:01:16,599 --> 00:01:22,079
ensconced on his bed of straw under a lantern which hung from a beam.

19
00:01:22,079 --> 00:01:27,200
He was 12 years old and had lately grown rather stout, but he was still a

20
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:33,239
majestic looking pig with a wise and benevolent appearance, in spite of the

21
00:01:33,239 --> 00:01:37,480
fact that his tushes had never been cut.

22
00:01:37,480 --> 00:01:42,040
Before long, the other animals began to arrive and make themselves comfortable

23
00:01:42,040 --> 00:01:44,640
after their different fashions.

24
00:01:44,640 --> 00:01:49,959
First came the three dogs, Bluebell, Jessie, and Pincher.

25
00:01:49,959 --> 00:01:56,080
And then the pigs, who settled down in the straw immediately in front of the platform.

26
00:01:56,080 --> 00:02:01,759
The hens perched themselves on the window sills, the pigeons fluttered up to the rafters,

27
00:02:01,759 --> 00:02:08,039
the sheep and cows lay down behind the pigs and began to chew the cud.

28
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:15,639
The two cart horses, Boxer and Clover, came in together, walking very slowly and setting down

29
00:02:15,639 --> 00:02:21,840
their vast hairy hooves with great care, lest there should be some small animal

30
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:24,639
concealed in the straw.

31
00:02:24,639 --> 00:02:29,639
Clover was a stout, motherly mare approaching middle life who had never

32
00:02:29,639 --> 00:02:34,080
quite got her figure back after her fourth foal.

33
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:41,560
Boxer was an enormous beast, nearly 18 hands high, and as strong as any two

34
00:02:41,560 --> 00:02:44,719
ordinary horses put together.

35
00:02:44,719 --> 00:02:49,879
A white stripe down his nose gave him a somewhat stupid appearance,

36
00:02:49,879 --> 00:02:57,759
and in fact, he was not of first-rate intelligence, but he was universally respected for his

37
00:02:57,759 --> 00:03:03,400
steadiness of character and tremendous powers of work.

38
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:09,840
After the horses came Muriel, the white goat, and Benjamin, the donkey.

39
00:03:09,840 --> 00:03:15,879
Benjamin was the oldest animal on the farm and the worst-tempered.

40
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:22,360
He seldom talked, and when he did, it was usually to make some cynical remark.

41
00:03:22,360 --> 00:03:27,800
For instance, he would say that God had given him a tail to keep the flies off,

42
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:32,919
but that he would sooner have had no tail and no flies.

43
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:37,439
Alone among the animals on the farm, he never laughed.

44
00:03:37,439 --> 00:03:42,680
If asked why, he would say that he saw nothing to laugh at.

45
00:03:42,680 --> 00:03:48,800
Nevertheless, without openly admitting it, he was devoted to Boxer.

46
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,039
The two of them usually spent their Sundays together in the small paddock

47
00:03:53,039 --> 00:03:59,120
beyond the orchard, grazing side by side and never speaking.

48
00:03:59,120 --> 00:04:03,919
The two horses had just lain down when a brood of ducklings, which had lost their

49
00:04:03,919 --> 00:04:09,680
mother, filed into the barn, cheeping feebly and wandering from side to side

50
00:04:09,680 --> 00:04:13,160
to find some place where they would not be trodden on.

51
00:04:13,160 --> 00:04:17,720
Clover made a sort of wall round them with her great foreleg and the ducklings

52
00:04:17,720 --> 00:04:21,759
nestled down inside it and promptly fell asleep.

53
00:04:21,759 --> 00:04:27,040
At the last moment, Mollie, the foolish, pretty white mare who drew Mr. Jones's

54
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:32,560
trap, came mincing daintily in, chewing at a lump of sugar.

55
00:04:32,560 --> 00:04:37,040
She took a place near the front and began flirting her white mane, hoping to

56
00:04:37,040 --> 00:04:40,720
draw attention to the red ribbons it was plaited with.

57
00:04:40,720 --> 00:04:45,918
Last of all came the cat, who looked round, as usual, for the warmest place

58
00:04:45,918 --> 00:04:50,160
and finally squeezed herself in between Boxer and Clover.

59
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:54,759
There she purred contentedly throughout Major's speech without listening to a

60
00:04:54,759 --> 00:04:57,079
word of what he was saying.

61
00:04:57,079 --> 00:05:02,279
All the animals were now present except Moses, the tame raven, who slept on a

62
00:05:02,279 --> 00:05:05,000
perch behind the back door.

63
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,959
When Major saw that they had all made themselves comfortable and were waiting attentively,

64
00:05:09,959 --> 00:05:14,000
he cleared his throat and began.
